\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Romania cenbank gov-prefer  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  to find own level   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Krista Hughes   \par \uc2 598 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 2007\par 19:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 ZURICH, June 22 (Reuters) - Romania's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is concerned that the strength of the leu will prove unsustainable but would prefer the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to find its own level, its governor said on Friday.   \par \par \uc2 In an interview with Reuters, Mugur Isarescu played down a jump in the leu to a fresh 4-1/2 year high against the euro on Friday, and said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was focused on longer-term trends.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a last resort, although always an option.   \par \par \uc2 "As long as it's possible to not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Romania\b0 \highlight\uc2  would like the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to find it's own level," he said. "But we have a managed float and we could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any time, if necessary."   \par \par \uc2 Isarescu confirmed earlier comments that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the leu's rise unless it was "deeply concerned", but declined to name a precise level which would set alarm bells ringing.   \par \par \uc2 "We are concerned generally that levels will prove to be unsustainable," he said. "I don't know if I am deeply concerned and I can't say exactly when I would be deeply concerned."   \par \par \uc2 "One day's movement is not enough ... let's see where we are on Monday or Tuesday."   \par \par \uc2 Isarescu said policymakers would take the rise in the leu into account in their June 25 interest rate decision but declined to say whether it made another cut more likely.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has cut benchmark interest rates by 150 basis points so far this year, in part due to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  worries, to 7.25 percent.   \par \par \uc2 "It's one element but the key element is our forecast for inflation as so far its well within target," he said.   \par \par \uc2 In the longer term, however, inflation pressures could reappear. "Wages are rising more rapidly than productivity growth, I do not consider this is sustainable," he said.   \par \par \uc2 NO RUSH FOR EURO   \par \par \uc2 Isarescu said the government's recent pledge to cut the budget deficit was a sound move in the lead-up to preparations to adopt the euro early next decade.   \par \par \uc2 "This is very welcome, it will be good for inflation and for the sustainability of economic growth, it's good for the current account deficit in a period of such rapid economic growth that the budget deficit should be as low as possible."   \par \par \uc2 But he said Romania was not in a hurry to adopt the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Bulgaria, which also joined the European Union in January, hopes to join euro proving ground ERM-2 this year but Romania is aiming for 2010-2012.   \par \par \uc2 "We are not in a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board so we have no urgency," Isarescu said. "Personally I do not consider it good to enter into a race to adopt the euro."   \par \par \uc2 He declined to comment on renewed calls for a change to the inflation test for euro entry.   \par \par \uc2 Isarescu said Romania had the necessary rules and regulations in place for fully-functioning capital markets and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was doing its bit by keeping inflation low.   \par \par \uc2 "I am sure that this time next year we will have a well-functioning capital market in Romania," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Romania has a small stock exchange and a nascent bond market with virtually no secondary trade.   \par \par \uc2 Some analysts say it needs to create more opportunities for foreign players eager to invest their cash in long-term instruments to help offset a vast current account deficit.   \par \par \uc2 ROMANIA/CENBANK (INTERVIEW)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RDN|BNX   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070622e36m001ki\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
