\par\par \b \uc2 Short-term outflows cannot hurt Romania-c.banker   \b0\par\par\uc2 356 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Mai 2007\par 15:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BUCHAREST, May 25 (Reuters) - Romania's financial stability is not threatened by current short-term capital inflows, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Mugur Isarescu said on Friday, calling the level of hot cash in the country "reasonable".   \par \par \uc2 Analysts have warned Romania could be at risk of sudden capital outflows if its gaping current account deficit widened sharply or there were signs that foreign investment cash is not sufficient to cover the shortfall.   \par \par \uc2 "We have 2 billion euros in short-term capital by non-residents, mostly bank deposits. If we compare that with any indicator, they are at a reasonable level," Isarescu told a banking seminar.   \par \par \uc2 Foreign investors have snapped up Romania's leu \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has firmed 3 percent against the euro since the start of the year, when Romania joined the European Union. Most of the inflows land in deposits or the vibrant real estate market.   \par \par \uc2 Hot cash is the equivalent of around 2 percent of last year's gross domestic product and some 10 percent of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.   \par \par \uc2 "I don't see a serious problem if that amount leaves ... we have a liquidity surplus in the market and we continue to sterilise ... Making a quick assessment of a current account correction based on monetary levers, risks are limited," Isarescu said.   \par \par \uc2 The current account deficit, which poses a major risk for Romania's fast growing economy, more than doubled in the first quarter compared with the same 2006 period. It was covered 43 percent by foreign investment.   \par \par \uc2 The International Monetary Fund sees the deficit widening to around 12 percent of GDP this year against 10 percent in 2006.   \par \par \uc2 Isarescu said sharp outflows can potentially trigger a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in theory.   \par \par \uc2 "If capital outflows occur, a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bank can take that into accont and possibly react through the rate policy. If that cannot temper the amplitude of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation, I believe that a bank can take into account a potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But I'm not making any projections."   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 ROMANIA\b0 \highlight\uc2 -C.BANK/|LANGEN|RDN|GNN|ABN|M|D|BNX|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070525e35p001hg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
