\par\par \b \uc2 EBRD-Romanian c.banker expects tight monetary stance  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Justyna Pawlak  \par \uc2 657 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2006\par 00:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, May 21 (Reuters) - Romania's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BNR) will maintain its hawkish interest rate stance for a while but its message to markets is "balanced," deputy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Cristian Popa on Sunday.  \par \par \uc2 Speaking on the sidelines of the annual meeting of the European Bank for Reconstruction and Development in London, Popa told Reuters high credit growth and stubborn inflation required tight monetary policy but he declined to comment if a new rate hike was in the cards.  \par \par \uc2 He said the bank was sending a "balanced" message to domestic markets, which had been rattled in recent weeks after rapidly changing their interpretation of a series of BNR comments.  \par \par \uc2 "We are saying: 'do not rejoice' regarding inflation," Popa told Reuters in an interview.  \par \par \uc2 "We are continuing the hawkishness from February ... We have been tightening policy since November. This continues by keeping the broad monetary conditions tighter for a longer period than envisaged in February," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The BNR, which adopted inflation targets last year, raised official lending costs by 100 basis points to 8.5 percent in February and has continued to mop up excess liquidity from the interbank market.  \par \par \uc2 But it kept interest rates on hold for two meetings in a row afterwards, including earlier this month, and some analysts said its inflation-fighting credibility was on the line because of forecasts of higher price growth in coming months.  \par \par \uc2 STUBBORN INFLATION  \par \par \uc2 Romanian inflation fell to a post-communist low of 6.9 percent on the year in April and is widely expected to rise in coming months due to tax hikes, before subsiding slightly later this year.  \par \par \uc2 The BNR has recently raised its end-year forecast from 6.5 percent to 6.8 percent and further above the December target of 4-6 percent.  \par \par \uc2 But Popa defended the bank's decision to keep rates unchanged, saying the mopping up of excess liquidity helped create tight monetary conditions, and said the bank was no longer as concerned about possible gains in the leu \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to rate hikes as it was last year.  \par \par \uc2 "What we are saying is that we are keeping monetary conditions tight even as disinflation progresses," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "We are also seeing the elimination of excess demand not at the end of 2006 anymore but in the beginning of 2007, which means tightening is required ... We will not have a cut for some time. But I am not sure about a hike," Popa said.  \par \par \uc2 Some analysts have said the BNR has not been raising interest rates aggressively this year despite stubborn inflation because of continued worries about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 Last year, such concerns drove the BNR to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  massively in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the leu in check. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  still gained some 7 percent in 2005 and is widely expected to continue appreciating this year.  \par \par \uc2 "Those concerns are much less pronounced than last year. We have not been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The preoccupation with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength remains, but it is far weaker than the preoccupation with inflation," Popa said.  \par \par \uc2 He added, echoing recent comments by BNR governor Mugur Isarescu, that the bank did not rule out further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets "if exceptional conditions warrant it, for example large swings in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange rates."  \par \par \uc2 "We can \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and we could do it in either direction ... But the pressure is still towards appreciation of the leu. What we don't want is unsustainable appreciation accumulating because when the market shifts that could result in sudden depreciation and that could hurt disinflation."  \par \par \uc2 Popa also said a recent weakening of the leu against the euro was temporary.  \par \par \uc2 "The recent weakening of the leu happened in an international context but the leu was one of the least hit \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and it has already rebounded. So the weakening does not require \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.  \par \par \uc2 EBRD-\b \highlight22\uc2 ROMANIA\b0 \highlight\uc2 -C.BANK (INTERVIEW)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|RNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060521e25l000ip\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
