\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 Romania \highlight\uc2  c.bank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  to stem leu rise-dealers  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Antonia Oprita  \par \uc2 450 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2005\par 12:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds analysts' quotes, background)  \par \par \uc2 BUCHAREST, Sept 12 (Reuters) - Romania's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Monday, buying euros to temper the leu's rapid firming and signalling to markets it had its eye on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers and analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 It was the first time the bank stepped into the market after last month's repeated attempts to tame the leu, just after announcing a switch to inflation targeting to fight rising prices as the country moves closer to European Union membership in 2007. Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BNR) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  first via commercial banks in the morning, when the leu was trading around 3.4760 bid, and brought it down to 3.5000, and then directly.  \par \par \uc2 "I think it bought around 50 million euros and wants the leu to stay between 3.4900 and 3.5000," said one dealer with a foreign bank.  \par \par \uc2 Others said the bank may have bought up to 70 million euros.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not comment. It has made clear the switch to inflation targeting -- seen as a system which uses interest rates rather than the exchange rate to fight inflation -- did not mean it would stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not comment on its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," spokesman Mugur Stet said.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers and analysts had expected the bank, which does not favour a fast leu appreciation, to step into the market as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  firmed last week, fuelled by export-related and some speculative hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wanted to show the market it was still watching its moves," said financial analyst Florian Libocor. "A fast leu appreciation is not justified because it doesn't come from a rise in value-added exports but from various other hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows."  \par \par \uc2 Exporters have complained that the fast leu appreciation was cutting their profits and even forcing them to go out of business, with low value added products such as textiles, furniture and footwear still taking a big part of exports.  \par \par \uc2 The leu, which opened at 3.4700/75 and reached a high of 3.4640/69 in morning trade, currently trades at 3.4800/70 to the euro.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say the bank, which targets end-year inflation of 7.5 percent with a one percent band on either side, may have to tread carefully between trying to contain a gaping current account deficit and controlling price rises.  \par \par \uc2 "At some stage, they (the BNR) will have to allow the leu to appreciate, because of inflation," said HVB analyst Simon Quijano-Evans.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ROMANIA (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|RDN|GNN|BNX|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050912e19c000va\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
