\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECTED - ANALYSIS-Foreign investors turned on by Romania.  \b0\par\par\uc2 893 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Dezember 2004\par 15:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 In London story headlined "ANALYSIS-Foreign investors turned on by Romania, fuel \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rally" please read in paragraph 7 "the key deposit rate is at 17.75 percent" ... instead of ... "the key deposit rate is at 18.25 percent." (corrects level of deposit rate).  \par \par \uc2 A corrected version follows  \par \par \uc2 By James Thornhill  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Dec 7 (Reuters) - It's difficult for foreign investors to get exposure to Romania's soaring \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but that hasn't stopped some of them and the attraction of high yields and EU entry in 2007 means more are likely to follow.  \par \par \uc2 The leu is up around six percent against the euro since November 3 - when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced a shift to a flexible managed float \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime - driven, at least in part, by speculative foreign inflows.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Romania\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively on Monday to stem the leu's charge, but analysts say it can probably only slow the pace of gains, given the growth of interest in Romania.  \par \par \uc2 Romania's benchmark Eurobond maturing in 2012 also underlines the strength of interest, with the risk premium having dropped to 4.0 percent, from 6.285 percent in May.  \par \par \uc2 Investors' confidence that Romania will remain on track for EU entry has insulated markets against a bout of political turbulence in the wake of last month's inconclusive parliamentary elections.  \par \par \uc2 "In spite of capital restrictions, Romania has benefited from the plentiful global liquidity chasing yield and, with better macro policies, approaching EU membership and high domestic rates, money has moved in," said Erik Nielsen, head of new European markets research at Goldman Sachs.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reduced participation in the market since November has encouraged leu bulls, salivating at the prospect of Romania's high yields - the key deposit rate is at 17.75 percent - and the convergence anchor of European Union entry in 2007.  \par \par \uc2 "People are looking for vehicles to express their bullish leu view. Interest rates are only a couple of percent below Turkey, but Romania is a lot closer to EU entry. From that perspective it's very attractive," said Koon Chow, emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at CSFB.  \par \par \uc2 Domestic traders corroborate the view from outside: "Foreign inflows are picking up and are set to grow on expectations of further liberalisation of capital flows, maintaining upward pressure on the leu," said one senior dealer in Bucharest.  \par \par \uc2 STRICT DOOR POLICY  \par \par \uc2 The attraction of Romanian markets to foreign investors are obvious. The trouble is, as Chow at CSFB puts it, it's still "hard as hell" for foreigners to buy leu assets and get in and out of the market easily.  \par \par \uc2 "It's definitely nowhere near as liquid as other parts of central Europe, there is an NDF (non-deliverable forward) market, but investors tend to take very small positions," said Ivailo Vesselinov, emerging markets analyst at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein.  \par \par \uc2 NDF markets allow investors to take forward positions in non-convertible or thinly traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , like the leu, with contracts settled in hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "They're still testing the waters, but we expect the market to develop quite rapidly over the next year," Vesselinov added.  \par \par \uc2 That development should be enhanced by the gradual lifting capital controls, in line with EU entry requirements.  \par \par \uc2 Romania has promised to allow foreigners access to leu deposits starting next April, with the full liberalisation of the capital account to take effect in time for EU membership in 2007.  \par \par \uc2 Bankers in London say hedge fund involvement in leu markets so far has been very limited, but that could change if these markets are opened up.  \par \par \uc2 "If these opportunities were there with an open market, people would be a lot more interested," said Goldman's Nielsen.  \par \par \uc2 FDI PICKING UP  \par \par \uc2 It's not just speculative investors who are taking a greater interest in Romania. A string of privatisations has helped fuel a strong pick up in foreign direct investment this year, with the trend set to continue.  \par \par \uc2 FDI inflows into Romania have badly lagged other countries in former communist eastern Europe since 1990, with investors deterred by weak institutions, red tape and widespread corruption.  \par \par \uc2 But a corner may have been turned in 2004, thanks to progress on economic reform and Romania finally gaining "functioning economy" status from the EU.  \par \par \uc2 "We haven't seen any particular FDI inflows since the (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) change, most of it has been speculative inflows. But the outlook for next year is quite positive, driven by ongoing progress towards EU entry," said Vesselinov at Dresdner.  \par \par \uc2 Progress on privatisation is indicative of the improved investment climate.  \par \par \uc2 The lower house of parliament approved last month the sale of leading oil company Petrom (SNPP.BX), two gas distributors and two electricity companies, all to foreign firms.  \par \par \uc2 Austrian energy group OMV (OMVV.VI) is expected to pay around 1.5 billion euros to take a 51 percent stake in Petrom by December 20, in a landmark deal that will double total FDI into Romania to 3.3 billion euros this year.  \par \par \uc2 Political uncertainty has so far failed to dampen investor enthusiasm for Romanian assets.  \par \par \uc2 The ruling ex-communists lost their majority in the poll, but could remain in power via an alliance with ethnic Hungarian parties. Romania also holds a presidential election on Dec. 12, which Prime Minister Adrian Nastase is favourite to win.  \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Mirela Roman in Bucharest).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041207e0c70091i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
