\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING: Poland Releases Oct Food Prices  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,187 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 2004\par 07:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Emerging Markets Report\par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par  \par Local/GMT \par  \par 0900/0800 Hungary October Unemployment \par  \par 0900/0800 Hungary Ctrl Bank Gov Jarai Press Briefing \par  .1000/0900 Poland October Unemployment \par           Seen at 18.7% from 18.9% in September \par  .1000/0900 Poland October Retail Sales \par           Seen up 6.7% on year from 8.8% in September \par  \par 1200/1100 Hungary Auctions HUF25B Of 10-Year Bonds \par  \par 1200/1100 Hungary Auctions HUF35B Of 5-Year Bonds \par  \par 1600/1500 Poland November Food Prices \par  \par 1300/1200 Czech Republic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rate Meeting \par           No rate change expected \par  \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par         Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                    2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par EUR/PLN 4.2100-40  4.2016-56  +0.20  4.2135  4.1785  -10.54 \par EUR/HUF 244.54-98  244.66-15  -0.05  244.88  244.86   -6.38 \par EUR/CZK 31.002-43  31.002-43   0.00  31.005  31.040   -3.72 \par EUR/SKK 39.217-67  39.217-67   0.00  Untraded today   -4.80 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 THE POLISH ZLOTY: Retail sales, unemployment and food prices will be of interest Thursday. Bank BPH expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue strengthening into the first quarter of next year, on a sharp jump in portfolio inflows since European Union entry in May, and the traditional pre-Christmas gains from repatration of funds by workers abroad.  \par \par \uc2 The zloty firmed Wednesday to PLN4.1990-.2090 to the euro from PLN4.2133 late Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 THE HUNGARIAN FORINT is likely to strengthen to HUF243.90-244.00 to the euro after the interest rate cut earlier this week, a local trader said.  \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed at HUF244.42.-.92 to the euro, barely changed from levels earlier in the day.  \par \par \uc2 THE CZECH KORUNA: Local traders expect the koruna's appreciation to slow ahead of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate meeting Thursday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to stay around CZK31.0 to the euro, with a possible weakening later this week.  \par \par \uc2 The koruna closed marginally stronger Wednesday, at CZK30.97-31.05 to the euro, from CZK31.02 late Tuesday, boosted mainly by a strong three-year bond auction.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLISH BONDS: food prices will be of interest Thursday as they are a good overall indicator of the inflation trend. The rise in the first half of November is expected to have slowed to 0.4% on the month, from 1.2% in the previous report, according to a Dow Jones Newswires poll.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Average yields on Polish treasury bonds fell after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hinted at the possibility of a rate hike Wednesday. The yield on the five-year bonds slipped by two basis points to 6.63%, and the yield on two-year bonds was down one basis point at 6.67%. A local trader said bonds were boosted by the monetary policy council's statement suggesting inflation may turn out to be even lower than projected in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's latest forecast.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARIAN BONDS: auctions of HUF35 billion of five-year and HUF25 billion of 10-year bonds will be the main focus Thursday. A local trader said the market will be saturated with longer-end bonds and prices are likely to fall.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yields on long-term Hungarian bonds slipped 10 basis points ahead of Thursday's auctions. Three-year bonds yielded 9.08%, five-years 8.93% and 10-years 7.95%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH BONDS are likely to have a calm session Thursday, local traders said, as some foreign investors will be out of the market because of the U.S. Thanksgiving holiday. Bonds are likely to trade sideways after recent steady price rises, they added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Wednesday, yields on the benchmark 15-year paper continued to drop as the strong koruna fueled steady demand for local bonds. The finance ministry's auction of three-year bonds was heavily oversubscribed, with offers for CZK11 billion, of which CZK3 billion were accepted. The average yield was 3.22%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par STOCKS \par  \par WIG20   1797.45       gain   1.8         up 0.1% \par BUX    14100.00       loss 110.97        dn 0.78% \par PX-D    2507.3        gain  26.5         up 1.60% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN WARSAW, the market is likely to slide Thursday in the absence of U.S. investors, local traders said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Wednesday, copper producer KGHM fell 2.6% as bank PKO BP sold a 5% stake in the company, CA IB Securities said. The sale overshadowed news that the firm will start mining its new Polish site in December and that it has been shortlisted to buy copper deposits in Peru from Monterrico Metals PLC. Software company Prokom came under pressure as minority shareholders sold some of their stake in the company because of an ongoing dispute with Prokom's owner. It closed down 2.8% on the day. The banking sector was under pressure from news earlier in the week that ING plans an initial public offering of its local unit.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN BUDAPEST, investors are likely to continue focusing on companies paying high dividends, such as utility firm Demasz, as Monday's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  interest rate cut means they return higher yields than other investments, said Peter Tordai, analyst at K&H Equities.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Wednesday, Demasz rose 4% at one point but closed the day up 2.4%. MOL was up 0.8% on the day, after a strong rally Tuesday prompted by its acquisition plans in Romania and an upgrade. The company's shares are likely to trade in a range between HUF12000 and HUF13000 over the next few weeks, or until there is evidence that its fourth-quarter results will be strong, at which stage it should rise further, said Tordai.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN PRAGUE, the focus will remain on CEZ after its results Wednesday, a local trader said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prague stocks closed higher Wednesday, with CEZ leading the pack. CEZ rose 2.52%, after it reported a rise in third-quarter net profit to CZK2.9 billion, from CZK1.5 billion a year earlier. Cesky Telecom rose 1.82% after local newspapers reported that Switzerland's Swisscom and Denmark's TDC A/S are seriously interested in taking over the government-owned telephone operator. A local trader said this makes it more likely the government would consider a capital market placement of its share rather than an outright sale. [ 25-11-04 0640GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OVERNIGHT NEWS \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Polish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Wednesday that it is reducing its 2005-2006 inflation projections by 30 basis points, leaving no doubt that easing inflation pressure was behind its decision to keep interest rates steady earlier Wednesday. Furthermore, the bank's rate-setting Monetary Policy Council, or RPP, said it believes the actual inflation risks are even lower than the reduced CPI forecasts indicate, because of the zloty's appreciation and an October fall in real wages.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Documents purported to be transcripts of top-level ruling party meetings have surfaced ahead of \f28 