\par\par \b \uc2 Romania approves law to slash four zeros from bills.  \b0\par\par\uc2 236 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juni 2004\par 16:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BUCHAREST, June 29 (Reuters) - Romania's parliament on Tuesday approved a law which will lighten the burden on shop assistants and book keepers by creating a new "heavy" leu worth 10,000 times the present national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 With the present "light" leu trading at around 40,700 to the euro (33,630 to the dollar), the reform has been long awaited.  \par \par \uc2 The heavy leu will circulate from July 2005 to the end of 2006 alongside its light counterpart, and light leus will then remain exchangeable for heavy ones at commercial banks until 2009.  \par \par \uc2 Romania is not the only country to be re-denominating a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ravaged by the effects of past heavy inflation.  \par \par \uc2 In January, the Turkish parliament approved a law to create a new lira worth a million times the present unit which currently trades at not far short of 1.5 million to the dollar. The new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will come in at the start of next year.  \par \par \uc2 Romania's leu, convertible on current account deals since 1998, will be fully liberalised also for capital account deals in 2007, when the ex-communist state hopes to join the European Union. Romanian officials have said the Balkan country will be able to adopt the euro sometime between 2012 or 2013.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Romania\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a managed float system, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  only in case of increased volatility.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040629e06t001vy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
