\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Romania's rush to free capital flows brings risks. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mirela Eremia \par \uc2 595 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2003\par 17:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUCHAREST, May 28 (Reuters) - With its sights set on joining the European Union in 2007, Romania is pulling down barriers to the flow of capital, but analysts warn rushing deregulation before truly reforming its ex-communist economy may leave it vulnerable. \par \par \uc2  Liberalising the movement of capital is a key part of the Balkan state's drive to win the EU's "functional market economy" tag - a signal that it is ready to move closer to the wealthy bloc. \par \par \uc2  Romania began freeing up various capital flows in 2001. Next year it plans to admit foreign securities on domestic markets, and to make it easier for foreigners to operate local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits in Romanian banks. \par \par \uc2  Freeing up the most sensitive flows - \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  with short term money market instruments and current and deposit accounts of residents abroad - is left for when Romania joins the EU. \par \par \uc2  The leu, convertible on current account deals since 1998, will be fully liberalised on the capital account in 2007, the year Romania hopes to join the EU. \par \par \uc2  But analysts say this may open the leu to speculative attack, a scenario already fought off this year by fellow EU aspirant Hungary, even though \b \highlight22\uc2 Romania\b0 \highlight\uc2  too could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  under its flexible exchange rate system. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think our economy is ready for such a calendar," former reform minister Ilie Serbanescu said. "We may have a wonderful exchange rate management and billions in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, but our economy is not prepared." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  runs a managed-float system, letting the leu move according to market demand, but \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in case of increased volatility. \par \par \uc2  "We're rushing a bit with some deregulation," said analyst Liviu Voinea, noting the poor monitoring of private debt flows and the absence of limits on the use of state securities as collateral for short-term borrowing by residents abroad. \par \par \uc2  GRADUAL PROCESS \par \par \uc2  Analysts also said high inflation, low levels of financial intermediation by banks between borrowers and lenders, weak law enforcement and possible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation posed ongoing risks. \par \par \uc2  "Even though Romania's economy recovered over the past years and a series of macroeconomic indicators look good, there are persistent vulnerabilities that cannot be ignored," said Daniel Daianu, an economics professor and former finance minister. \par \par \uc2  Inflation halved to 17.8 percent last year and could be down in single digits from next year. The budget deficit target is 2.65 percent of GDP this year - well within the Maastricht criteria for euro zone membership, though the most optimistic date for Romania joining the euro single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is 2010. Bank and government officials admit structural reforms must be in place to prevent a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis, but insist capital account liberalisation is seen as both gradual and prudent. \par \par \uc2  "For each stage there will be a careful assessment...and the EU allows \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if there are disturbances," said \b \highlight22\uc2 Romania\b0 \highlight\uc2 's chief EU negotiator Vasile Puscas. \par \par \uc2  Valentin Lazea, chief economist at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , noted that most foreign inflows into Romania were direct investments - seen at $1.4 billion this year, up from $1.1 billion in 2002, when Romanians working abroad sent back $1.25 billion. \par \par \uc2  He said falling real interest rates on Romanian bonds would also make speculation less attractive. \par \par \uc2  "All other (EU) candidates freed up capital flows four to five years ahead of accession," he said. "To postpone that calendar indirectly means to assume a delay in accession." \par \par \uc2  "But, if Romania does not tackle all the other aspects of (EU) integration, the risks are greater." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030528dz5s00uv8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
